  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nA cold front is located NW of Nashville this evening, with a band of \nshowers located along it.The front will be on the Plateau around \n12Z, and exit the area to the east around 18Z. Rainfall amounts will \ngenerally be around a quarter inch in the northern half of the area, \nwith a trace to a tenth for southern sections. Showers and clouds \nmay persist into the afternoon in the mountains as a NW flow \ndevelops and low level moisture lingers, trapped against the \nmountains. Drier air at 850 mb arrives tonight, clearing out any \nremaining clouds and showers. \n\nThe next system will affect the area on Wednesday through \nThanksgiving Day. A deep trough dives south across the Plains on \nTuesday night, inducing surface cyclogenesis near the FL/GA \ncoastline, with the low tracking north across eastern NC. The \nstrengthening 850 mb low over GA will bring an easterly LLJ of 40-50 \nkt from Wednesday evening through Thursday morning. The direction \ndoes not appear ideal for mountain waves, but some mountain areas \nmay have some strong gusty winds. Wind gusts in the forecast will be \nincreased above NBM values. We could get some much needed rainfall \namounts with this system, although the holiday timing isn't ideal. \nQPF amounts will be in the range of a half to one inch across the \narea for Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nNext weekend looks dry with near normal temperatures.",
